<main id="main">
<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/roof-shingles.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/roof-shingles-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/roof-shingles.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="526" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>Claim dictionary</nav><span class="kicker fade-up">Reference</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.08s">Insurance Claim Dictionary for Oregon Property Owners</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.15s">44 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English, with Oregon-specific entries.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the dictionary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “depreciation” or “umpire”"></div><p class="gloss-count" data-gloss-count aria-live="polite">44 terms</p><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. The first payment under most replacement cost policies.</dd><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs of living elsewhere while a home is repaired, such as rent and meals above normal.</dd><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of the damage. The only question an appraisal panel decides.</dd><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either side demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd><dt>Atmospheric river</dt><dd>A long band of moisture that brings heavy rain and sometimes strong wind to the Pacific Northwest.</dd><dt id="g-C">Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy condition that can reduce payment if the property was insured for less than a required percentage of its value.</dd><dt>Contents (Coverage C)</dt><dd>Coverage for personal belongings.</dd><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing limits, deductibles, coverages and the policy period.</dd><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The part of a covered loss the policyholder pays before the insurer pays.</dd><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age and condition. Often recoverable after repairs under replacement cost policies.</dd><dt>Disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser without a financial stake in the outcome beyond a fee, as most appraisal clauses require.</dd><dt>Division of Financial Regulation (DFR)</dt><dd>The Oregon agency, within the Department of Consumer and Business Services, that regulates insurance and handles consumer complaints.</dd><dt>Dwelling (Coverage A)</dt><dd>Coverage for the house itself and attached structures.</dd><dt id="g-E">Earthquake endorsement</dt><dd>Optional coverage added to a property policy for earthquake damage, usually with a percentage deductible.</dd><dt>Estimate</dt><dd>An itemized list of repair work with quantities and prices.</dd><dt>Extended replacement cost</dt><dd>An endorsement that pays above the dwelling limit, often by 20% to 50%, if rebuilding costs more than expected.</dd><dt id="g-F">FAIR Plan</dt><dd>A state-established insurer of last resort. In Oregon, the Oregon FAIR Plan Association.</dd><dt id="g-H">Holdback</dt><dd>Recoverable depreciation withheld until repairs are complete.</dd><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as a quantity of shingles or square feet of drywall.</dd><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>Whether new materials can reasonably match undamaged ones in color, texture and style.</dd><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Steps taken to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or water extraction.</dd><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of meeting building codes when repairing or rebuilding.</dd><dt>Other structures (Coverage B)</dt><dd>Coverage for detached structures such as garages, fences and sheds.</dd><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for coordinating a multi-trade repair, commonly 10% plus 10%.</dd><dt id="g-P">Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible calculated as a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat dollar amount.</dd><dt>Pitch</dt><dd>The steepness of a roof, written as inches of rise per 12 inches of run.</dd><dt>Policy limit</dt><dd>The maximum the insurer will pay under a coverage.</dd><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, often required within a set time after the insurer requests it.</dd><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A licensed professional who represents policyholders in handling and negotiating a claim for a fee.</dd><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation paid once repairs or replacement are completed.</dd><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer says it is handling the claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The description of what is damaged and what work is needed to repair it.</dd><dt>Smoke infiltration</dt><dd>Smoke and soot entering a structure from a nearby fire, including wildfire.</dd><dt>Square</dt><dd>100 square feet of roof surface.</dd><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An added labor charge for working on steep roofs, usually 7/12 pitch and above.</dd><dt>Subrogation</dt><dd>The insurer’s right to recover what it paid from a third party who caused the loss.</dd><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy’s deadline to file a lawsuit, commonly two years from the loss in Oregon.</dd><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for items missed or underpriced in the original estimate.</dd><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the appraisers can’t agree on.</dd><dt>Unfair claim settlement practices</dt><dd>Insurer conduct prohibited by statute; in Oregon, listed in ORS 746.230.</dd><dt id="g-W">Wildland-urban interface (WUI)</dt><dd>Areas where homes meet or mix with undeveloped wildland vegetation and face higher wildfire risk.</dd><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Widely used estimating software for property insurance claims.</dd></dl></article>
